ABSTRACT={This paper evaluates the deployment and potential nuclear fuel requirements of Small Modular Reactors (SMR) for supplying the electricity demand associated with data center expansion. The proposed approach is applied to Brazil as a reference scenario, for which an original database was developed based on Ministerial Ordinances issued by the Brazilian Ministry of Mines and Energy (MME) between 2020 and 2025 regarding requests to connect data center facilities to the National Interconnected System (SIN). From this survey, a reference electricity demand scenario of approximately 2.52 GW was constructed, concentrated mainly in the states of São Paulo and Ceará. Three Pressurized Water Reactor (PWR)-type SMR designs were comparatively evaluated using reactor core modeling and fuel-cycle-based metrics, including Initial Heavy Metal Mass (IHMM), burnup, and natural uranium requirements. The evaluated reactors were the ACP100, BANDI, and RITM-200N. The analysis combines OpenMC-based reactor inventory estimations with fuel utilization models to estimate the required number of reactor units and fleet-level uranium demand associated with SMR deployment. The results indicate significant differences among reactor designs regarding deployment scale, fuel inventory, and uranium consumption. The RITM-200N presented the lowest IHMM requirement, whereas its higher enrichment level resulted in the largest specific natural uranium demand per unit of electricity generated. Overall, the study provides a physically grounded framework for evaluating the deployment and material implications of SMR for data center electricity supply, which can be adapted to alternative demand scenarios as more precise or context-specific data become available.}}